Use less memory while compiling a large script, and optionally keep no
source text.

QuickJS parses a whole script before it finishes any function, so the
definition and phase 1 bytecode of every function are held at once.
For YouTube's 9.8 MB player bundle that peaked at 75.8 MB on the host.
This patch makes the following changes.

- A source position in phase 1 and 2 bytecode takes 5 bytes
  (OP_source_loc2, line << 16 | column) when the line and column are
  below 65536, 9 bytes (OP_source_loc) otherwise, and a position that
  directly follows another replaces it. Positions were a third of the
  phase 1 bytecode.
- The state of phases 2 and 3 (jump slots, source position slots, the
  pc2line buffer and its counters) moved out of JSFunctionDef into
  JSFunctionDefLate, which js_create_function keeps on its stack. A
  function definition is 472 bytes instead of 552 on a 64-bit host and
  356 instead of 408 on PowerPC.
- js_fd_trim runs once, after the function's return is emitted (the
  return used to grow the trimmed buffer by half again), trims the
  label table too, and shrinks with js_realloc (the DynBuf realloc
  function does not shrink).
- Function definitions, the compiler's arrays (js_realloc_array) and
  its bytecode buffers are allocated outside the small-block arenas.
  In the arenas they left holes between the atoms and small functions
  made at the same time (4.5 MB of free arena space after compiling the
  bundle). A block that already has room is kept, and a buffer starts
  at 64 bytes, so compiling is no slower.
- JS_EVAL_FLAG_NO_SOURCE compiles a script without keeping its source
  text. Line and column numbers are kept. Function.prototype.toString
  gives such a function the native function form with its kind
  ("function f() { [native code] }", "async function", "function *")
  and a class constructor "class C { }": YouTube tests
  /\bclass\b/.test(class{}.toString()) to choose how to construct
  classes. The kind is the new JSFunctionBytecode.text_dropped field,
  written as 2 more bits of the function flags.

The final bytecode is the same except that more jumps take their 8-bit
forms (pass 3 estimates jump sizes from the smaller phase 2 code: 155
bytes fewer over the bundle). Adding an opcode changes the bytecode
version hash. Compiling the bundle alone (2026-11-16, host): peak
75,831 KB before, 62,779 KB with the first three changes, 54,979 KB
with all of them; kept after compiling, 40,567 KB before and 25,351 KB
after.

